1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,932
This video explains the authentication architecture in the Test Automation Framework.

2
00:00:05,932 --> 00:00:18,875
The goal is to understand how password login, OAuth client credentials, static bearer tokens, API keys, and composite authentication fit into one execution model.

3
00:00:18,875 --> 00:00:23,189
We will stay at a practical architecture level.

4
00:00:23,189 --> 00:00:38,289
The examples come from the current framework implementation, and the emphasis is on the responsibilities of each class, the runtime flow, and what a test author actually writes.

5
00:00:39,089 --> 00:00:45,131
The design starts by separating two responsibilities that are often combined.

6
00:00:45,131 --> 00:00:50,623
A token provider answers where an access token comes from.

7
00:00:50,623 --> 00:01:04,354
It might call a username and password login endpoint, perform an OAuth client credentials grant, or return a token supplied by an external secret store.

8
00:01:04,354 --> 00:01:10,395
A request authenticator answers how credentials modify an outgoing HTTP request.

9
00:01:10,395 --> 00:01:14,240
A bearer authenticator adds the Authorization header.

10
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:19,732
An API key authenticator adds a header or query parameter.

11
00:01:19,732 --> 00:01:25,773
This separation lets several acquisition protocols feed the same bearer mechanism.

12
00:01:25,773 --> 00:01:31,815
It also keeps caching and refresh logic independent from request construction.

13
00:01:32,615 --> 00:01:37,069
The YAML contains two authentication catalogs.

14
00:01:37,069 --> 00:01:40,038
Token providers describe acquisition.

15
00:01:40,038 --> 00:01:43,007
Profiles describe request authentication.

16
00:01:43,007 --> 00:01:54,883
The checkout-user provider names the auth service, username, logical password reference, login path, and refresh path.

17
00:01:54,883 --> 00:02:03,048
The user-jwt profile points to that provider and declares bearer authentication.

18
00:02:03,048 --> 00:02:08,243
Services can then name a default profile.

19
00:02:08,243 --> 00:02:11,955
Secrets never appear in YAML.

20
00:02:11,955 --> 00:02:20,119
A logical reference such as checkout-user-password becomes the environment variable TAF_SECRET_CHECKOUT_USER_PASSWORD.

21
00:02:20,119 --> 00:02:30,511
ConfigLoader converts this data into immutable records and validates references before any test runs.

22
00:02:31,311 --> 00:02:46,311
At suite startup, SuiteConfigListener loads the selected environment, validates the complete catalog, constructs the authentication runtime, and stores both objects as TestNG suite attributes.

23
00:02:46,311 --> 00:02:56,311
AuthenticationRuntimeFactory builds one runtime provider for every named token provider, then builds every named request profile.

24
00:02:56,311 --> 00:03:04,436
Token caches are suite scoped, so parallel tests using checkout-user share one cache.

25
00:03:04,436 --> 00:03:11,311
A TestContext remains test scoped and carries correlation information, not tokens.

26
00:03:11,311 --> 00:03:22,561
BaseApiTest retrieves the shared runtime and creates an ApiClient for a named service and the current test context.

27
00:03:22,561 --> 00:03:29,436
This lifecycle avoids repeated login while keeping mutable test state isolated.

28
00:03:30,236 --> 00:03:37,698
For the ordinary user flow, order-service declares user-jwt as its default profile.

29
00:03:37,698 --> 00:03:43,916
When a test calls orders.create, the authentication runtime selects user-jwt.

30
00:03:43,916 --> 00:03:50,134
BearerAuthenticator asks the checkout-user access token provider for a token.

31
00:03:50,134 --> 00:03:56,974
On the first request, the caching provider invokes the password-login TokenAcquirer.

32
00:03:56,974 --> 00:04:11,898
That strategy creates an unauthenticated client for auth-service, posts LoginRequest to the configured login path, maps LoginResponse, and converts it into a framework TokenGrant.

33
00:04:11,898 --> 00:04:18,737
The bearer authenticator then adds Authorization colon Bearer plus the token.

34
00:04:18,737 --> 00:04:23,712
The test never requests or passes that token.

35
00:04:24,512 --> 00:04:30,735
CachingAccessTokenProvider keeps the token lifecycle out of both tests and request authenticators.

36
00:04:30,735 --> 00:04:39,550
A normal request reads the volatile cached value and returns immediately when the token is still usable.

37
00:04:39,550 --> 00:04:43,699
A stale or missing value enters a lock.

38
00:04:43,699 --> 00:04:52,514
The cache checks again after acquiring the lock because another test thread may already have refreshed it.

39
00:04:52,514 --> 00:04:58,737
This produces single-flight behavior: many parallel requests cause one acquisition or refresh.

40
00:04:58,737 --> 00:05:09,627
The provider refreshes early, using ten percent of the token lifetime with a safety margin bounded between one and thirty seconds.

41
00:05:09,627 --> 00:05:13,257
Password login can use its refresh token.

42
00:05:13,257 --> 00:05:19,998
OAuth client credentials has no refresh token, so it performs a new acquisition.

43
00:05:20,799 --> 00:05:24,716
The automation-client provider represents a machine identity.

44
00:05:24,716 --> 00:05:35,346
Its public client ID and requested scopes remain in YAML, while the client secret comes from the credential source.

45
00:05:35,346 --> 00:05:46,536
AuthApi sends the token request with HTTP Basic credentials and a form body containing the client credentials grant and scope.

46
00:05:46,536 --> 00:05:51,013
OAuthTokenResponse becomes a TokenGrant with no refresh token.

47
00:05:51,013 --> 00:05:57,727
The service-oauth profile uses the same BearerAuthenticator used by the password flow.

48
00:05:57,727 --> 00:06:02,203
The only change is the AccessTokenProvider behind it.

49
00:06:02,203 --> 00:06:08,357
Near expiry, the shared cache calls acquire again rather than refresh.

50
00:06:08,357 --> 00:06:16,750
An API operation can own the service-oauth profile so normal tests only call the operation.

51
00:06:17,550 --> 00:06:23,386
StaticAccessTokenProvider supports environments where another system already issues the token.

52
00:06:23,386 --> 00:06:27,472
Configuration stores only a token credential reference.

53
00:06:27,472 --> 00:06:35,059
Suite startup resolves the value and constructs a provider that returns it unchanged.

54
00:06:35,059 --> 00:06:39,144
The same BearerAuthenticator attaches it to requests.

55
00:06:39,144 --> 00:06:45,564
This provider has no expiry calculation, refresh call, or JWT decoding.

56
00:06:45,564 --> 00:06:52,567
Secret rotation becomes visible only when a new suite runtime is constructed.

57
00:06:52,567 --> 00:07:05,991
Static tokens are useful for controlled external integrations, but a renewable provider is safer for long suites when the authentication service is available.

58
00:07:05,991 --> 00:07:14,745
The sample catalog supports this type, although it does not currently declare a static provider.

59
00:07:15,545 --> 00:07:22,951
ApiKeyAuthenticator receives the resolved key, its placement, and the configured parameter name.

60
00:07:22,951 --> 00:07:26,037
Header placement can produce X-API-Key.

61
00:07:26,037 --> 00:07:34,061
Query placement can append an encoded api_key parameter without rebuilding unrelated query values.

62
00:07:34,061 --> 00:07:40,850
In both cases, an explicit value supplied by the caller wins.

63
00:07:40,850 --> 00:07:53,811
That rule is important for negative tests because an intentionally invalid key must not be replaced by the valid configured key.

64
00:07:53,811 --> 00:08:04,303
The API key is a request credential, so it does not need an AccessTokenProvider or token cache.

65
00:08:04,303 --> 00:08:12,327
The current test-api-key profile reads TAF_SECRET_TEST_API_KEY and attaches it as an X-API-Key header.

66
00:08:13,127 --> 00:08:17,341
CompositeAuthenticator applies every member in declaration order.

67
00:08:17,341 --> 00:08:26,370
The configured user-jwt-and-api-key profile first applies the bearer authenticator and then the API key authenticator.

68
00:08:26,370 --> 00:08:37,807
Each member receives the immutable request copy returned by the previous member, so credentials accumulate on one outgoing request.

69
00:08:37,807 --> 00:08:41,419
Composite means all requirements, not fallback.

70
00:08:41,419 --> 00:08:50,448
The framework does not send one request with bearer and retry with an API key.

71
00:08:50,448 --> 00:08:57,070
ConfigLoader verifies member names, rejects duplicate members, and performs cycle detection.

72
00:08:57,070 --> 00:09:07,303
The runtime factory then recursively materializes the already validated graph and reuses authenticators that were built earlier.

73
00:09:08,103 --> 00:09:13,457
AuthenticationRuntime resolves one effective profile with deterministic precedence.

74
00:09:13,457 --> 00:09:16,802
An explicit request profile wins.

75
00:09:16,802 --> 00:09:20,148
Otherwise the operation profile wins.

76
00:09:20,148 --> 00:09:23,494
Otherwise the service default applies.

77
00:09:23,494 --> 00:09:28,848
If none exists, the request has no authentication.

78
00:09:28,848 --> 00:09:32,863
DISABLED is different from ordinary inheritance.

79
00:09:32,863 --> 00:09:40,224
It deliberately suppresses operation and service defaults, which enables missing-authentication tests.

80
00:09:40,224 --> 00:09:51,600
The framework does not infer profiles from paths and never tries another profile after a rejected response.

81
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:58,961
Stable policy normally belongs to the API operation or service configuration.

82
00:09:58,961 --> 00:10:04,984
Request-level selection should remain an exception for authentication-specific tests.

83
00:10:05,784 --> 00:10:11,076
ApiClient prepares the neutral HttpRequest before executing interceptors.

84
00:10:11,076 --> 00:10:17,691
When an AuthenticationRuntime exists, it prepends AuthenticationInterceptor for that request.

85
00:10:17,691 --> 00:10:30,260
The interceptor passes the request selection, operation profile, service default, and TestContext to AuthenticationRuntime, then forwards the authenticated request.

86
00:10:30,260 --> 00:10:41,506
Common headers and correlation follow, and logging runs after those mutations so diagnostics describe the final request.

87
00:10:41,506 --> 00:10:45,475
Sensitive values must therefore be redacted.

88
00:10:45,475 --> 00:10:51,429
The transport remains unaware of profiles and token providers.

89
00:10:51,429 --> 00:11:04,659
Token endpoint clients deliberately omit AuthenticationRuntime, preventing an infinite cycle in which obtaining a token would itself require a token.

90
00:11:05,459 --> 00:11:10,149
The intended test experience is intentionally boring.

91
00:11:10,149 --> 00:11:21,538
A normal order test creates a context, creates OrderApi for order-service, calls create, and asserts the response.

92
00:11:21,538 --> 00:11:28,907
The service default supplies user-jwt without token plumbing in the test.

93
00:11:28,907 --> 00:11:32,257
Negative tests retain explicit control.

94
00:11:32,257 --> 00:11:35,607
disableAuthentication sends no framework credentials.

95
00:11:35,607 --> 00:11:45,656
Supplying an Authorization header preserves an invalid token because BearerAuthenticator does not overwrite caller input.

96
00:11:45,656 --> 00:11:50,346
The same principle applies to API keys.

97
00:11:50,346 --> 00:12:03,745
Tests own expected status codes and business assertions, while authentication infrastructure owns secret access, acquisition, caching, refresh, and request modification.

98
00:12:04,544 --> 00:12:12,629
The framework uses Strategy for token providers, token acquirers, and request authenticators.

99
00:12:12,629 --> 00:12:16,672
AuthenticationRuntimeFactory acts as the composition root.

100
00:12:16,672 --> 00:12:22,062
AuthenticationRuntime is a named registry with deterministic policy.

101
00:12:22,062 --> 00:12:30,821
CompositeAuthenticator implements the Composite pattern, and AuthenticationInterceptor joins the HTTP chain of responsibility.

102
00:12:30,821 --> 00:12:36,212
The design follows single responsibility and dependency inversion.

103
00:12:36,212 --> 00:12:39,580
It also sets conservative boundaries.

104
00:12:39,580 --> 00:12:41,602
Tokens remain opaque.

105
00:12:41,602 --> 00:12:47,666
A four-oh-one does not trigger automatic refresh or replay.

106
00:12:47,666 --> 00:12:54,404
The runtime does not guess a scheme from the URL.

107
00:12:54,404 --> 00:13:05,184
Those choices prevent hidden retries, accidental duplicate side effects, and authentication defects that appear to pass.

108
00:13:05,984 --> 00:13:19,033
The existing tests cover direct login and refresh behavior, service-default JWT bearer calls, authenticated checkout, missing authentication, and invalid bearer tokens.

109
00:13:19,033 --> 00:13:28,975
The repository also configures OAuth, API key, and composite profiles and provides AuthenticationScenarioApi methods for them.

110
00:13:28,975 --> 00:13:37,053
However, the current TestNG suite does not appear to invoke those scenario methods.

111
00:13:37,053 --> 00:13:42,024
Static token support is implemented but not configured.

112
00:13:42,024 --> 00:13:49,480
Focused unit tests for cache timing and single-flight concurrency are also missing.

113
00:13:49,480 --> 00:14:00,665
The architecture is coherent, but verification should be expanded around those paths before treating every scheme as proven.

114
00:14:01,465 --> 00:14:04,661
Keep four words in mind.

115
00:14:04,661 --> 00:14:09,135
Provider means where a token comes from.

116
00:14:09,135 --> 00:14:13,609
Profile means how credentials modify a request.

117
00:14:13,609 --> 00:14:16,805
Runtime means which profile applies.

118
00:14:16,805 --> 00:14:20,640
Interceptor means where authentication joins execution.

119
00:14:20,640 --> 00:14:27,031
Password login, OAuth, and static tokens all supply bearer authentication.

120
00:14:27,031 --> 00:14:30,227
API keys modify requests directly.

121
00:14:30,227 --> 00:14:35,340
Composite profiles apply several authenticators to one request.

122
00:14:35,340 --> 00:14:45,567
Configuration defines stable policy, suite startup constructs shared runtime state, and ordinary tests express business intent.

123
00:14:45,567 --> 00:15:01,546
With that model, each class has a clear place and adding a future authentication mechanism becomes a deliberate extension rather than another conditional inside ApiClient.
